So in this question we have to remove duplicates from a sorted vector and return the size of the new array quite confusing wording .
So what I did in this one is I used the unique vector method to get all the unique elements to the left of the vector .
unique() returns an iterator pointing to the new logical end of the unique elements.
and then we erase the elements form that iterator to the end of the vector.

code---

class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        nums.erase(unique(nums.begin(),nums.end()),nums.end());
        return nums.size();
    }
};
Time complexity --
unique() → O(n) — it traverses the array once.
erase() → O(n) in the worst case — elements may need to be shifted.
return nums.size() → O(1).
total = O(2n) -> O(n)
